{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Moses said, etc. הֲצֹאן וּבָקָר, etc.</b> The words astonish me coming from the one who said them, for it is not fitting to say such a thing even from a person who did not recognize the greatness of the Elohim of Israel, and all the more so from a man of Elohim. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sifrei) — some of them considered it a sin for him, and some of them said that he was astonished at the falling of Israel: why should six hundred thousand, etc., die for the sake of meat. And the homiletical interpretation may be expounded, but in any case one must settle the plain sense of the words. One must further know why he went back to say the things stated <b>וְאַתָּה אָמַרְתָּ בָּשָׂר אֶתֵּן</b>. One must further know what wonder is this — <b>הֲצֹאן וּבָקָר</b>, etc. — for even without a miracle they have livestock to sustain them from it for a month's time, and the verse speaks at length (Exodus 12:38): ״וְגַם עֵרֶב רַב וְגוֹ׳ וְצֹאן וּבָקָר״, etc. One must further know why he exaggerated so greatly with all the fish of the sea — that all the fish of the sea would need to be gathered to supply them for a month's time — for does not the whole world eat fish that are twice as many all those times over compared to the six hundred thousand that Israel were?" "<b>Truly</b>, the verses are resolved in two ways. The first: when HaShem said to him that He would give them meat without specification, Moses understood the implication of the words to mean that He would give them one kind of meat. Therefore he said before Him <b>six hundred thousand</b>, etc. — meaning, a multitude as great as this will be found among them with differing desires: this one will desire sheep meat, and this one cattle, and this one ram and deer, and this one fowl, and this one fish — <b>וְאַתָּה אָמַרְתָּ בָּשָׂר אֶתֵּן לָהֶם</b>, implying one kind of meat. What is the meat that You will give? <b>הֲצֹאן וּבָקָר וְגוֹ׳ וּמָצָא לָהֶם</b> — meaning, that which is sought — <b>אִם אֶת כָּל דְּגֵי</b> — meaning, kinds of fish, etc. — <b>וּמָצָא לָהֶם</b> — meaning, that which is sought. For when You give them sheep and cattle meat they will say they desire ram and deer, and when You give them ram and deer they will say they desire fowl meat, and when You give them fowl they will say fish. And he marked the two extremes of meat: sheep and cattle, and the final extreme — fish meat, and the intermediate gradations between sheep-and-cattle meat and fish meat are self-understood. And he said <b>כָּל דְּגֵי הַיָּם</b> — meaning, that it would not suffice with only some kinds of fish unless He brings all kinds of fish that are in the sea, for lacking this, the missing kind — they will weep over it. From this point on, there is no stopping their mouths short of every kind of meat of domesticated and wild animals and every kind of fowl and every kind of fish sufficient for them for a month's time. You thus find that the core of his wonder was on account of his understanding from the words of HaShem that it would be one kind of meat, and it is not so — had He said He would give them every kind of meat in the world, he would not have been astonished. And through this all the difficulties are resolved, and the words of Moses find favor. And the principal intent of Moses in this may perhaps be to prevent the giving of the meat, so that the children of Israel would not die, for HaShem made this dependent on this." "<b>And a</b> second way, according to their words of blessed memory (Berakhot 58a) who said that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not perform a miracle for falsehood. And inasmuch as HaShem said to him that the meat would be their sword, according to their words of blessed memory (Numbers 11:20) in Rashi's explanation, ״וְהָיָה לָכֶם לְזָרָא״ — for so they call, etc., a sword of zara — if so, the verse is not speaking of a miracle but that they would eat from the meat that is available. And regarding this he wondered that there was not available for them enough to eat for thirty days. And the statement <b>בָּשָׂר אֶתֵּן לָהֶם</b>, which appears to mean that it would be given anew, means that He would give them permission to eat it, for until now they did not have permission to eat from their livestock, and they were dedicated to the service of HaShem. And HaShem said that He would give them permission to eat meat. And the statement <b>אִם אֶת כָּל דְּגֵי הַיָּם</b> — meaning, because they had fish from the well that traveled with them, but they did not have sufficiency even for one day in the month. When Moses assessed what was needed for six hundred thousand for thirty days, he assessed that all the fish that could possibly be present there would not suffice. And by way of miracle, we have already said that he held that HaShem would not perform a miracle for the purpose of an evil outcome, as I have written. And according to this way, Moses' wonder is a genuine wonder and bears no blame whatsoever, for it does not imply any deficiency in the capability of HaShem, heaven forbid."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:00.192727148Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}